<p>Venezuela deported Colombian businessman and former industry minister Alex Saab to the United States on Saturday, May 16, the Venezuelan migration authority SAIME said, adding that he faced criminal proceedings there. SAIME described Saab as a Colombian citizen and said the measure followed Venezuelan migration law because he was “incurso en la comisión de diversos delitos en los Estados Unidos de América”. Saab, 54, was a close ally of Nicolás Maduro and held senior posts after returning to Venezuela from U.S. custody in December 2023, including president of the International Center for Productive Investment and, later, minister of Industry and National Production. He arrived at Opa-locka airport in Miami-Dade County on Saturday evening escorted by U.S. federal agents, including DEA personnel, according to EFE accounts carried by Venezuelan and regional media.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XZP8XkxZRFtv9IyOikw6uVJJOPHK_RNvZG3an2dBWwnzSHZrpSsciKoOu2UP8_nM42PKDp-2JCZIB61DZhiWOvVlejr9mL6iblGm1Z2mWiL3tRs1URjATWwb6g9qXX_7f1Lf0aNNy-7ZeiH4YLExNGaf4ohDtvF-t3ZMDcTGzM-fIjTF2YJbMq2TbTt_5Xpj' alt='Alex Saab, center, in Caracas, Venezuela, in 2023, was once among key allies of Nicolás Maduro.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Political shift: Reports described the handover as part of Delcy Rodríguez’s interim government’s wider action against figures tied to Maduro.</li><li>Pre-handover uncertainty: Several reports said Saab had fallen from favor after Maduro’s ouster, and local accounts described rumors of his detention or unclear whereabouts before the announcement.</li><li>U.S. allegations: U.S. authorities have accused Saab of money laundering and corruption tied to Venezuelan government contracts, including food-program contracts under CLAP, according to accounts of the U.S. cases.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Venezuelan opposition figures: Opposition figures celebrated the transfer and said Saab could become an important witness in the case against